course: biology
lesson: 24
title: Wolves and dogs
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Every pet dog, from a tiny chihuahua to a Great Dane, comes from wolves that began living alongside people more than 15,000 years ago; people kept the friendliest ones, and over thousands of years they became dogs.
explain: A wolf pack is usually just a family, a mother and father with their young, and in deep snow they trot in single file, stepping in each other's tracks to save energy.
explain: Your dog keeps the wolf's tools: a cold wet nose with hundreds of millions of smell receptors, ears that turn to catch the car coming home before anyone else hears it, and a tongue for panting because dogs hardly sweat.
explain: Cartoons show wolves howling at a full moon and a Big Bad Wolf hunting children; real wolves point their heads up so the howl carries kilometres to the pack, not at the moon, and wild wolves are usually very shy of people.
fact: Wolves live in packs that are usually a family: a mother, a father and their young of different ages.
fact: The idea of an alpha wolf fighting its way to the top came from captive wolves; wild packs are mostly parents leading their family.
fact: Wolves howl to tell the pack where they are and warn other packs away, and a howl can carry several kilometres; they do not howl at the moon.
fact: Wolves chase deer, elk and bison and pick the weak, old or sick, yet most hunts, often more than eight in ten, fail.
fact: Wolves returned to Yellowstone National Park in 1995 after about 70 years; elk grazed less by the rivers, trees grew back and beavers returned, a chain called a trophic cascade.
fact: All pet dogs come from wolves tamed probably more than 15,000 years ago, and a chihuahua and a Great Dane are the same species.
fact: A dog's nose has hundreds of millions of smell receptors, while a human nose has about 5 or 6 million.
fact: A dog's nose is wet with a thin layer of mucus that catches smell particles, and every dog's nose print is unique.
fact: Dogs hardly sweat through their skin, so they cool down by panting: water dries off the tongue and takes heat with it, which is called evaporation, and it works less well on a humid day, when the air is already full of water; their only real sweat glands are in their paw pads.
fact: A loose wide tail wag usually means a happy dog, but a stiff high wag can mean the dog is tense.
fact: Dogs hear sounds much quieter and higher than people can and turn their ears with many muscles.
fact: Dogs see mostly blues and yellows because they have two kinds of colour cells instead of our three, so red and green look much alike to them.
fact: Never approach a strange dog or a wild animal, because even friendly-looking ones may bite if scared or protecting food or young; to stroke a dog, ask its owner first and let the dog sniff your hand.
exercise: What is a wolf pack usually made of? | A family: parents and their young | A crowd of strangers who fight to lead | Only adult males | Wolves of many different species
exercise: Why do wolves walk in single file through deep snow? | Stepping in each other's tracks saves energy | They are hiding how many they are from the moon | The leader won't let the others walk beside her | Wolves can only see straight ahead
exercise: Why do wolves howl together at night? | To keep in touch with the pack and warn other packs away | Because the moon hurts their eyes | To call the moon closer | Because they are cold and hungry and want somebody to bring them dinner
exercise: Which animal does a wolf pack usually try to catch from a herd of elk? | A weak, old or sick one | The biggest and strongest bull | Always the leader of the herd | Whichever one is nearest to the pack
exercise: About how many wolf hunts end with no catch? | Most of them, often more than eight in ten | Almost none of them, wolves are perfect hunters | Exactly half of them | About one in a hundred
exercise: What happened after wolves came back to Yellowstone in 1995? | Elk grazed less by rivers, so trees grew back and beavers returned | The rivers dried up completely | All the elk disappeared from the park within one winter, and the trees died | Nothing changed at all
exercise: What is the chain of changes caused by a top hunter called? | A trophic cascade | A food pyramid | A wolf wave | A predator storm
exercise: What animal did all pet dogs come from? | The wolf | The fox | The hyena | The jackal and the bear together
exercise: About how many smell receptors does a human nose have, compared with a dog's hundreds of millions? | About 5 or 6 million | About 500 million | About 50 | About 5 billion
exercise: Why is a sniffer dog useful at an airport? | Its nose can find tiny traces of a scent | It can see straight through the sides of suitcases | It can hear what is inside bags | It can taste the air for metal
exercise: Why is a dog's nose wet? | A thin layer of mucus catches smell particles | The dog has just been drinking water, every single time | It is sweating through its nose | Its nose is always a bit ill
exercise: How does a dog cool down after running? | It pants, so water evaporates from its tongue | It sweats all over its fur | It flaps its big floppy ears, just as an elephant does | It lies on its back and kicks
exercise: Where does a dog have its few sweat glands? | In the pads of its paws | On its tongue only | All over its back | Inside its ears
exercise: A dog does a stiff, high tail wag. What might it mean? | The dog is tense or alert | The dog is always happy | The dog is falling asleep | The dog is hungry
exercise: Why does a dog hear the car before anyone else? | It hears quieter and higher sounds than people | It feels the car with its nose | It has seen it from the window | It guesses, because the car always comes at that time
exercise: Why might a dog run past a red ball on green grass? | Red and green look much alike to a dog | Dogs see in black and white only | Dogs don't like red things and ignore them | The ball smells too much like grass
exercise: What does a nature reserve sign tell you to do about strange dogs and wild animals? | Never approach them | Feed them so they trust you | Run towards them to scare them | Pet them only on the head
test: everyone knows wolves howl at the full moon, like in the cartoons yeah? | No, they point up so the howl carries, not at the moon | Yes, the full moon makes them howl at it, every night of the month | Yes, but only in winter | No, wolves never howl at all
test: My mate says the pack leader is the toughest alpha who beat the others. What do scientists say now? | Wild packs are mostly parents leading their own family | Yes, the leader must fight and win a new battle every day | The leader is chosen by howling loudest | Wolves have no leaders, they never meet up
test: A chihuahua and a wolf in a picture side by side. Which is true? | Both come from the same ancestors; the chihuahua is a bred wolf descendant | They are not related at all | The chihuahua came from a fox | The wolf came from the chihuahua
test: Count them: a pack of five wolves trots past. How many legs and how many tails? | 20 legs and 5 tails | 10 legs and 5 tails | 20 legs and 10 tails | 25 legs and 5 tails
test: What would happen to the trees by Yellowstone's rivers if the wolves were taken away again? | Elk would graze them more and fewer would grow | The trees would grow faster than ever | The rivers would freeze over and stay frozen all year | The beavers would eat the elk
test: Why might a blue toy be a better buy for your dog than a red one? | Dogs see blue well, but red looks dull to them | Dogs hate the smell of the red paint used on toys | Red toys are too heavy for dogs | Dogs are afraid of blue things
test: In the Big Bad Wolf story the wolf hunts people. What is real? | Wild wolves are usually very shy of people, and attacks are rare | Wolves hunt people every night | Wolves only eat grandmothers who live alone in little houses in the forest | Wolves live in houses in the forest
test: Why could a dog nose print work like a fingerprint? | Every dog's nose has its own unique pattern of ridges | All dogs have exactly the same nose | Nose prints change every day | Dogs leave prints only with their tails, never their noses
test: get your hands off that dog, you don't even know whose it is | Right, ask the owner first and let the dog sniff you | Wrong, all dogs love being grabbed | Right, because dogs never bite anyone | Wrong, strangers' dogs are always safe to touch and to grab
test: A dog has been trained to find people buried in snow after an avalanche. Which sense does it use most? | Smell | Sight | Taste | Hearing only
test: Why is panting a good way for a dog to cool down, but not much use on a very humid day? | It works by evaporation, and wet air slows evaporation | Humid air makes dogs' tongues smaller | Dogs stop breathing on humid days | Panting only works in winter
exam: Identify the animal from which, according to the established evidence, all domestic dogs are descended. | The grey wolf | The red fox | The spotted hyena | The brown bear
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the composition of a wild wolf pack? | It consists principally of a breeding pair and their offspring | It consists of unrelated males contending for dominance | It consists solely of adult females, the males living apart from the pack | It consists of several distinct species
exam: Ascertain the principal mechanism by which a dog dissipates excess bodily heat. | Panting, whereby moisture evaporates from the tongue | Profuse sweating across the entire skin | The flapping of the ears | The shedding of the coat
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning colour vision in the domestic dog. | It perceives chiefly blues and yellows, and confuses red with green | It perceives nothing but shades of grey | It perceives more colours than a human does, including red and green | It perceives red more vividly than any other colour
